                  "day": ""
               }
            }
         ],
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "#### Terrain\n\nIn every subtraction, squares represent the digits. When the top digit is smaller than the bottom digit, the column borrows 1 from the next order: ten squares are grouped, the bottom digit is removed from that group, and the squares left in the group are the complement to ten. So, before you start subtracting two digits, ask yourself: is the bottom digit greater than the adjusted top digit — the top digit reduced by one when the previous column borrowed 1? That answer tells you whether the column will need to borrow 1.",
               "source": [],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            }
         ],
         "desc": null,
         "next": "97b26e04-5bac-4698-992b-f064e0ed2ca6",
